h(18, 300, """The worst I have seen of the scenes of people's world: baseness!""")

t(18, 785, """In the matter of people trying to inherit the properties of others, it is not permissible to take the ruling: "Acting for the sake of Allah, so the one who asks is not repulsed and small kindnesses are not withheld from him"; this is a deduction like "a hip on a rib that do not fit together" and does not make the ruling straight. For acting for the sake of Allah the Exalted is certain, fixed and obligatory in everything within ability; repulsing the one who asks is forbidden and not right to do, and withholding small kindnesses is most strictly warned against for the one who does it. As for my post yesterday, it was a direction to a group of people in every age and land not to try to inherit people while alive in what of their wealth was not made lawful for them. This has nothing to do with the comment that came on the post, in which the honourable, virtuous brother built a meaning on which the structure of the legal ruling does not stand straight. With the command to act for the sake of Allah the Exalted, and with the decisive prohibition of repulsing the one who asks, it is not right that the asker turns into one who demands a right in what he has no right to! For our Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was the most generous of people, the most noble of people, the most open-handed of people and the greatest of them in giving, but he was not one to legislate for people a right that was not imposed on them by others. So people should not be obliged with what they were not obliged with. A man's neighbour today reaches out to him for two onions of the four in his kitchen; how does the asker, who does not want to be repulsed and does not want his neighbour's giving withheld from him, know that his neighbour with children has the price of new onions to buy for his children?!""")

h(18, 1550, """A meaning is not built on a deduction that creates "a hip on a rib". Be utterly careful when you cite the words of Allah as evidence, and do not raise your own meaning over verses!""")

t(18, 2110, """If you give him an onion, he desires your whole kitchen; if you honour him with the use of your car once, he wants it every day; and if you do him a kindness with something, he thinks he has a right in you! All of this is from foolishness, bad character and little intelligence. For the people of kindness are the people of kindness even if their kindness is half a date; kindness is established for them, and not harming them becomes obligatory; otherwise oppression has occurred, and one has strayed from the straight path!""")

h(18, 2400, """Among the worst of people: those who try to inherit the people of virtue!""")

t(19, 575, """The wronged has authority on earth

One of the most beautiful things I like to take up from time to time is exhortative ideas that address the most painful side of people's lives, which is the issue of oppression between the oppressor and the oppressed; not to explain the sin of oppression specifically, for it is among the matters clear to people, but I take up the matter to explain the nearness of the servants of Allah the Mighty to their Lord, and so that we truly see how far that tremendous cosmic distance, which we imagine visually between us and our Mighty Lord, melts away and disappears. Perhaps then we will stop looking at the sky and being affected by that distant visual expanse toward its heights, and we will understand, realise and know with certainty that the distance is not real, and that we, actually and not figuratively, by the measure of the power of Allah our Mighty Lord and the truth of the comprehensive cosmic realities, are not as most humans imagine.

In reality nothing separates us from Allah the Exalted: no distance, no remoteness, nor all this that the human mind imagines when it looks at the sun, the moon and the stars, so its owner visually imagines that what his eyes see is a far distance; while the true root, proven in structure and meaning, is that the distance is zero!

🟢 A doctoral student whose supervisor tormented him over his thesis for twelve years, until he gave up seeking the degree. Then the thesis supervisor died, and the student was among those escorting his funeral. The grave cast him out for about an hour, and his family, standing at the grave with the people, said: Whoever has a grievance against him, let him forgive him. The student tormented by the supervisor's bad conduct says: "I said: O Lord, I have forgiven, for Your noble Face." So the grave accepted him.

🟢 Another suspected that someone had wronged him severely, but he could not ascertain it, and the suspected one was in distress. One day the wronged one said: "O Allah, I forgive him only in this world." The distress lifted from him at once that very day.

The examples showing the nearness of our Mighty Lord to us cannot be counted, no counter can count them, and none knows their number and abundance but the Lord of the wronged, the Companion of the weak and the Mender of the broken. How then are prayers answered like this, and how do words arrive in the blink of an eye, while we still think there are billions of light years between us and our Lord?!""")

h(19, 1800, """The authority of the wronged is a reality""")

t(19, 2330, """When I criticise the Muslim brother Adnan Ibrahim, I criticise him only because I reject a great deal of his thought, affected by the intellectual vessels that he allowed to shape his self-awareness; this shaping affected the balance of his ability to unpack what is hard to grasp of the light of the Muhammadan Message, since the influence of dozens of intellectual vessels on Adnan's mind produced in him a state of not taking sides in favour of the rules of deduction with the impartiality that must be fully realised for a reflecting mind without the influences of the vessels of diverse human thought. This established in Adnan's experience a research method whose rules are not disciplined in the data of the precise balance of the originality and uniqueness of abstract Islamic values and concepts, without which it is impossible to set up a structure that escapes crookedness, and this is what our brother in Islam did not reach, not to mention his being alone in much at the level of his journey full of right things…""")

t(20, 95, """And apart from all this, I was stopped by his attack when he was asked the reason for his silence about the events in his country, so he attacked the other side. This is not keeping away from falling into fitnah, but rather being struck by it; for when a person is asked the reason for his silence, the most correct for him is to stay silent to escape the pit of taking sides; if he answers by attacking one of the sides, he has taken sides by aligning, whether he wished or not!" """)

