t(21, 1470, """There are peoples and nations that see, in their ethnic partnership, in one skin colour and in a shared destiny, a loyalty that rises to the level of embracing a religion and far exceeds the value of loyalty; even though they are peoples in which ritual theological irreligion, even "wholly creedal" irreligion for a great number of them, has eaten away, they have been cohesive since 1945 in partnership to the point of "religiosity toward one another". Their strong feeling for one another has become a religion around the texts of their partnership and unity, like religious hymns, with the power of the guiding words of a creed when its verses are recited. The Japanese who committed suicide at Pearl Harbor was a religiously fanatical devotee, because Japan was the religion for him; he was devoted to Japan. I say religion, not loyalty to Japan, because loyalty is never enough for a Japanese soldier to throw his body into a fire blazing in battles!

The European popular component today is very religious in the system of shared European destiny, to the point that this system has acquired the power of a religion's influence!

So the influence on the masses is at the level of religiosity and the level of the power of religion, not the level of loyalty; that influence is real, and it is no exaggeration that we describe the matter as religiosity, because it has exceeded the limits of loyalty!

Will the matter stay like this? That is another matter, not the subject of the post.""")

h(21, 2300, """99.9 of humans are very religious! Religiosity is not limited to religion! Loyalty to race, colour and language is religiosity!""")

t(22, 380, """Happiness does not mean zero sadness! Happiness is a value tested in contentment at the time of pain. If happiness were in the self, then look around you at the examples of the people of corruption: they put on a show of happiness, then they are exposed to people amid weeping and wailing, complaining to people of their pains despite the wealth and fame they have, and showing them, with riches and status, the truth of what was hidden of their condition. Where is the happiness of their selves, while their bodies enjoy luxurious houses, they wear clothes slow to wear out compared with the clothes of the poor, and they ride mounts unlike people's mounts?! So happiness is not in the self, and the matter is that most people search for it in the wrong place, so they never find it. As long as people do not understand the true meaning of happiness, and keep thinking it is money, a strong passport, status, a villa and a luxury car, they will keep inventing a happiness of incorrect meaning and value, not the happiness the knowers know. Happiness certainly does not mean hunger, nor living in places fit only for chickens and sheep, nor wearing shabby, dirty clothes. Happiness has nothing to do with wealth or with poverty, nothing near or far to do with comfort or toil; these are earthly human concepts that humans invented, rooted and built on, so they led themselves astray and wandered with the wanderers. Happiness is a completely different value. The happiness of riches and health, and the sadness of poverty and illness, are values and meanings that explain the outward phenomena of life and deal with the changes of the dispositions of fate as reactions. Happiness is something else, far from linking good and evil, wealth and need, relief and worry. Happiness is a value buried in the spirit, having absolutely nothing to do with any of those things! It is that single, unique, wondrous, miraculous value bound to Allah without interruption; it is not affected by wealth that comes or is cut off, nor by comfort that comes or goes, nor by relief that descends or is lifted. It is not tied to any of this, so I said: it has nothing to do with the self at all, and nothing to do with the dispositions of fate and its states, nor with the changes in the life of the servant in any state. So do not be part of the lie of popular happiness that man invented, set its wrong laws and conditions for, and lived in pain with what his human mind produced, suffering and struggling!""")

h(22, 1450, """The self is not made happy; that is an illusion man has perfected in making and believing! Happiness is a value buried in the spirit, not in the self!""")

t(22, 1970, """Peace be upon you, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings

New followers are coming to the channel, and in any case they are honourable brothers alighting in this gathering for the first time, so welcome to them, no doubt. Their questions have increased, and my answer is only by way of notice, since they always ask: From which books do you copy? Or in whose words among the shaykhs do you write in your posts?! And to these honourable ones I say: any post that I have not ended or begun with the word "copied" is not copied from a scholar, a shaykh, a jurist or a thinker, earlier or later; for I write, by the grace of Allah the Exalted, by reflection, not from memorisation or study, and I never sat one day before a shaykh to receive knowledge, with my respect for every man of knowledge. I present what I reflect on to "the Book and the Sunnah", understanding and reading by myself, and I find it, the near whole of it, 99.9, in agreement with them, illumined by their light; then I broadcast it and publish it to people.

As for what the revelation does not mention explicitly and verbally, with a clear "wording", of the sweet basil of thought and the daisies of the waymarks of the road to the Lord of the Worlds, which is not of the rulings of the religion nor of its legislation, but only of the doors of intimate converse with the Lord and attaining union with the Worshipped; I present that same reflection, morning and evening, to what I have understood through reading, comprehension and learning in tawhid, then I broadcast it, certain that it is in the heart of tawhid and in the very core of its centre, settled, fixed, clear, just, true, and obligatory to know for each of the believing men and believing women.

Allah blessed me two years ago with a dream that came about something of what I am speaking of, so I mention it by way of""")

t(23, 60, """"one thing brings up another", to clarify what may be unclear to a new follower of the category of attackers, so that if he is of those raised on the method of believing in true dreams his soul may be at ease, or he may pass on rightly guided. For I saw, as the sleeper sees, a rectangular table, noticeably long, its cover on top a clean white cloth, and the cloth covering its sides; a soft cover like silk, whose material I do not know, extremely white and pure. On top of it was a panel of very old cardboard, coloured, ornamented and decorated with a decoration whose like I have not seen, and the cardboard was firmly stuck to the body of the panel, a part of it and it a part of it, and it was mostly of the gold kind of paper, as though its material were gold. The panel was of two halves, like the first two pages in a copy of the Qur'an, placed on a small stand supporting it, and it was fixed on the table. As for the panel, its age was thousands of years, and on it Surat al-Fatiha was written in a script that is not Arabic, whose like I have not seen among today's languages despite their number. The Christians in the place admired it and asked about it, although they owned tables in the place with their books on them, and they were boasting of their books on the tables that belonged to them, as though the matter were an exhibition of religion, each group displaying what it owned. I owned only this panel, while they owned books of many kinds. When they came to me asking about the panel, I said: I inherited it from the Prophet ﷺ.

To the honourable new brothers: do not busy yourselves with the writer of the posts, the weak servant in need of Allah; this servant, long years ago, was among the most ignorant of humans in knowledge and understanding of Allah, and I have not related the journey because of how very strange it is, for today and tomorrow I see that the hope, at the beginning and the end, is reaching Allah the Exalted.""")

h(23, 950, """Notice""")

t(23, 1500, """What is reached by the spirit has no standard and no measure; some of it requires striving and comes as a reward, and some of it descends as pure grace from the Mighty Lord without hardship or striving.

The spirit has tremendous ability to rise up without the material of means. It is enough for you, in your place of prayer or in your seclusion, even if you are among people, to close your eyes and ears to what you see and hear, the closing of one who renounces the result, so that you separate as the one seeking refuge separates, and your spirit manifests in your body, and you fill it with the single items of truth, not with the collections of illusion. For every truth comes singly, and every illusion comes collectively; truth is taken from the mouths of individuals, while illusion is picked up from the tongues of the crowds. So take your truth and seek refuge with it in the sanctuary of salvation. It is a door open to every one of us that does not close; keeping away from it does not mean it is closed, and the one who shuns a thing does not prove that the thing does not want him! And as long as the gate of refuge from the world is wide open and never closes, the fault lies in the one who kept away and refused to come near, and this is the first truth.""")

h(23, 2200, """The capabilities of the self are zero, and the capabilities of the spirit are greater than man imagines!""")

t(24, 235, """In the abode of this world, the servant is aware with the awareness of obligation, which requires the self and memory, because behind the servant is a Day of Reckoning. In the abode of the Barzakh, where the believers are, the servant is aware with the awareness of honouring, which requires the spirit, not detailed memory, after leaving the abode of the Barzakh! For the memory of the Barzakh is cut off, while the memory of this world is settled and protected from erasure; because the self in this world is an enemy to the body and to itself, while the spirit in the Barzakh is the body's remedy after its suffering. So awareness in this world is a mixture produced by obligations, the servant in it between ease and hardship, joy and sorrow; while in the abode of the Barzakh awareness is an awareness of absolute rest and a journey of separation from toil and hardship, so awareness comes momentarily, with no need to recall its details until the Day of Resurrection, while knowing that the life of the Barzakh took place.

And Allah, my Lord and the Lord of people, is Highest, Most Majestic, Greatest and Most Knowing""")

